/Type /StructElem
/S /H4
/P 49 0 R
/T (Abstract This paper investigates the severe structural vulnerability of pre-trained Transformer architectures against semantic-preserving lexical substitutions in sentiment classification tasks. While state-of-the-art networks like DistilBERT achieve superior predictive accuracy on standard curated corpora, they exhibit catastrophic performance degradation when evaluated under localized word-level adversarial constraints. Utilizing the Stanford Sentiment Treebank \(SST-2\) benchmark dataset, we systematically expose a fine-tuned DistilBERT-base-uncased encoder to an automated Synonym Replacement Attack Framework. This framework isolates non-stopword tokens and dynamically substitutes them with lexical equivalents derived from the NLTK WordNet database. Under these baseline adversarial conditions, the model accuracy collapses to a fragile baseline stability metric of 49.00%, rendering the deep network no more reliable than a random uniform coin flip. To mitigate this optimization drift, we implement an innovative multi-epoch Adversarial Fine-Tuning Pipeline. By expanding the training distribution to incorporate a balanced matrix of both canonical inputs and dynamically perturbed synonym-injected sequences, we force the model's inner self-attention mechanisms to minimize local token-ID reliance and instead capture invariant, global semantic representations. Experimental results demonstrate a complete recovery of model stability over three optimization epochs, introducing zero degradation to clean benchmarking validation accuracy. Finally, a real-time web-based Gradio deployment layout is realized to validate the localized operational resilience of the fortified encoder network.)
/E (Abstract This paper investigates the severe structural vulnerability of pre-trained Transformer architectures against semantic-preserving lexical substitutions in sentiment classification tasks. While state-of-the-art networks like DistilBERT achieve superior predictive accuracy on standard curated corpora, they exhibit catastrophic performance degradation when evaluated under localized word-level adversarial constraints. Utilizing the Stanford Sentiment Treebank \(SST-2\) benchmark dataset, we systematically expose a fine-tuned DistilBERT-base-uncased encoder to an automated Synonym Replacement Attack Framework. This framework isolates non-stopword tokens and dynamically substitutes them with lexical equivalents derived from the NLTK WordNet database. Under these baseline adversarial conditions, the model accuracy collapses to a fragile baseline stability metric of 49.00%, rendering the deep network no more reliable than a random uniform coin flip. To mitigate this optimization drift, we implement an innovative multi-epoch Adversarial Fine-Tuning Pipeline. By expanding the training distribution to incorporate a balanced matrix of both canonical inputs and dynamically perturbed synonym-injected sequences, we force the model's inner self-attention mechanisms to minimize local token-ID reliance and instead capture invariant, global semantic representations. Experimental results demonstrate a complete recovery of model stability over three optimization epochs, introducing zero degradation to clean benchmarking validation accuracy. Finally, a real-time web-based Gradio deployment layout is realized to validate the localized operational resilience of the fortified encoder network.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
