154 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (To thoroughly evaluate the effectiveness of our adversarial training regime, we conduct extensive empirical testing using the Stanford Sentiment Treebank dataset, which serves as a core benchmark for binary text classification. This dataset provides a balanced distribution across its classes, containing sixty-seven thousand three hundred forty-nine samples in the training split, eight hundred seventy-two samples in the validation split, and one thousand eight hundred twenty-one samples in the final testing split. Maintaining this exact data split is essential, as it allows us to directly compare our model's performance against historical baseline metrics and ensure the integrity of our benchmarking evaluation. The dataset sentences represent a wide variety of writing styles, lengths, and linguistic complexities, making it a highly reliable playground for testing adversarial robustness in natural language processing tasks.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [41]
>>
endobj
155 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (The computational setup for these experiments uses an enterprise-grade cloud computing platform equipped with an NVIDIA Tesla T4 Graphics Processing Unit running active parallelized CUDA kernels. The foundational encoder is built upon the standard DistilBERT-base-uncased layout, which optimizes performance by using six transformer layers, twelve self-attention heads, and a hidden dimension size of seven hundred sixty-eight, totaling approximately sixty-six million active parameters. This architecture provides the ideal foundation for testing, delivering high-speed execution while retaining the deep representation capabilities needed to handle complex language variations. The system uses a specialized AdamW optimizer to apply decoupled weight decay, which helps control parameter growth during intense fine-tuning phases.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [42]
>>
endobj
156 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (To ensure steady convergence and prevent the model from overfitting on synthetic text modifications, the optimization process is governed by a strict hyperparameter matrix within the Hugging Face Trainer API. The system is configured with a training batch size of eight samples per device, an optimization run spanning three full epochs, and a target learning rate of $2 \\times 10^{-5}$ managed by the AdamW optimizer. Additionally, we apply a weight decay coefficient of $0.01$ as an L2 regularization constant to penalize large attention values, and set the checkpoint saving frequency to passive mode to maximize data throughput on the GPU. This balance prevents the model from shifting its entire weight matrix too far, ensuring it retains historical language knowledge while learning the new defensive patterns.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [43]
>>
endobj
157 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (The final evaluation metrics focus on tracking two prima)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [44]
>>
endobj
158 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 51 0 R
/E (ry indicators of system performance: clean classification validation accuracy and adversarial robustness stability scores. The baseline model shows strong performance on clean data, achieving an initial validation accuracy of ninety-one point twenty percent, but drops significantly to forty-nine point zero percent when tested against synonym attacks. In comparison, our fortified framework maintains high performance on clean data at ninety-one point fifteen percent, while successfully raising the adversarial robustness stability score to eighty-seven point sixty percent. This shows the fortified model can defend against lexical manipulation without losing its core predictive accuracy.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [45]
>>
endobj
