199 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 52 0 R
/T (Tokenization Mechanics, WordPiece Overlap, and Architectural Layers)
/E (Tokenization Mechanics, WordPiece Overlap, and Architectural Layers)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [40]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 52 0 R
/T (A thorough analysis of our defensive pipeline reveals that the model's robustness depends heavily on the interaction between the WordPiece tokenization sub-layer and the six architectural transformer layers of the distilled BERT encoder. The baseline tokenizer maps text sequences into discrete sub-word tokens using a fixed vocabulary of thirty thousand five hundred twenty-two unique character blocks. When a targeted keyword substitution occurs, the tokenizer often breaks down the new synonym into multiple fragmented sub-words, which introduces entirely new positional encodings and token indices into the high-dimensional embedding matrix. To illustrate this mechanical process, we can analyze the structural changes that occur within the network's layers when processing different text formats. In a standard setup, a clean target word like "excellent" is mapped directly to a single high-frequency token ID. If an attack swaps this word for a valid synonym like "splendid," the tokenizer maps it to a completely different, less frequent index. In the baseline model \(V1\), this unexpected index shifts the hidden state vector across the transformer layers, which causes the final feed-forward pooling layer to output an incorrect prediction. Our custom fortification approach addresses this issue directly within the model's internal layers. The six transformer layers inside our fine-tuned DistilBERT network use twelve parallel self-attention heads to compute a dynamic attention matrix across the entire sequence. The mathematical equation governing this self-attention mechanism is defined as follows: $$\\text{Attention}\(Q, K, V\) = \\text{softmax}\\left\(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d}_k}\\right\)V$$ Where $Q, K, \\text{and } V$ represent the Query, Key, and Value matrices derived from the token embeddings, and $d_k$ represents the scaling dimension of seven hundred sixty-eight. By training the network on a balanced mixture of both original and altered synonym strings, we systematically force the self-attention mechanism to adjust its attention weights. Instead of concentrating high attention scores onto specific token indices, the attention heads learn to distribute weight across surrounding context vectors, such as functional verbs and punctuation markers. This structural adjustment creates an invariant semantic space where different synonym tokens map to identical vector trajectories, allowing the model to accurately preserve its sentiment decisions regardless of vocabulary variations.)
/E (A thorough analysis of our defensive pipeline reveals that the model's robustness depends heavily on the interaction between the WordPiece tokenization sub-layer and the six architectural transformer layers of the distilled BERT encoder. The baseline tokenizer maps text sequences into discrete sub-word tokens using a fixed vocabulary of thirty thousand five hundred twenty-two unique character blocks. When a targeted keyword substitution occurs, the tokenizer often breaks down the new synonym into multiple fragmented sub-words, which introduces entirely new positional encodings and token indices into the high-dimensional embedding matrix. To illustrate this mechanical process, we can analyze the structural changes that occur within the network's layers when processing different text formats. In a standard setup, a clean target word like "excellent" is mapped directly to a single high-frequency token ID. If an attack swaps this word for a valid synonym like "splendid," the tokenizer maps it to a completely different, less frequent index. In the baseline model \(V1\), this unexpected index shifts the hidden state vector across the transformer layers, which causes the final feed-forward pooling layer to output an incorrect prediction. Our custom fortification approach addresses this issue directly within the model's internal layers. The six transformer layers inside our fine-tuned DistilBERT network use twelve parallel self-attention heads to compute a dynamic attention matrix across the entire sequence. The mathematical equation governing this self-attention mechanism is defined as follows: $$\\text{Attention}\(Q, K, V\) = \\text{softmax}\\left\(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d}_k}\\right\)V$$ Where $Q, K, \\text{and } V$ represent the Query, Key, and Value matrices derived from the token embeddings, and $d_k$ represents the scaling dimension of seven hundred sixty-eight. By training the network on a balanced mixture of both original and altered synonym strings, we systematically force the self-attention mechanism to adjust its attention weights. Instead of concentrating high attention scores onto specific token indices, the attention heads learn to distribute weight across surrounding context vectors, such as functional verbs and punctuation markers. This structural adjustment creates an invariant semantic space where different synonym tokens map to identical vector trajectories, allowing the model to accurately preserve its sentiment decisions regardless of vocabulary variations.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [41]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
