/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Despite the successful optimization convergence and high performance scores achieved by our custom defensive architecture, several prominent limitations remain within the current framework that must be explicitly detailed for thorough scientific evaluation. First and foremost, our current dynamic perturbation engine is entirely dependent on and constrained by static, predefined lexical lookup relational graphs, specifically the WordNet synset database. Because the text transformation loop relies on these fixed dictionary links, it remains highly vulnerable to complex, context-dependent modern slang, internet-born social terminology, creative typographical variations, and multi-word idioms that do not possess direct single-word synonym equivalents inside standardized lexical repositories. If an adversary uses modern text variations or out-of-vocabulary slang strings at inference time, the static lookup table cannot match them, causing our defense sub-layer to overlook the attack and pass the altered sequence directly to the transformer encoder.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [23]
>>
endobj
227 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Furthermore, our defense strategy is currently focused entirely on single-token substitutions and does not account for larger structural grammatical manipulations. For example, if an attacker changes the semantic layout of a sentence by converting it from active to passive voice, or inserts long, neutral words that confuse the model's self-attention patterns, our framework will struggle to defend itself. This happens because the model was trained to handle specific word swaps rather than complete structural layout changes.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [24]
>>
endobj
228 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Finally, the current setup applies text perturbations uniformly across the entire input string without calculating which specific words contribute most to the final output score. This lack of targeting means the system treats all non-stop words equally, which can sometimes lead to generating synthetic sentences that lack natural linguistic flow or introduce unnecessary semantic noise into the fine-tuning process.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [25]
>>
endobj
