231 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (To overcome the limitations discovered during this project, our future research directions will explore several advanced deep learning techniques designed to turn this static defense into an adaptive linguistic barrier. Moving forward, our primary objective is to replace the dictionary-dependent WordNet lookup engine with a dynamic, automated Generative Adversarial Network framework or a contextual masked language model, such as RoBERTa. By using a pre-trained language model to generate input variations, the system will be able to analyze the entire context of a sentence and produce highly natural, context-aware semantic perturbations in real time during the training cycle. This will allow the defense mechanism to move beyond simple word-for-word swaps and protect the model against a much wider range of sophisticated, real-world text variations.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [28]
>>
endobj
232 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (Additionally, we plan to implement gradient-based feature importance mapping to identify the exact tokens that have the highest impact on the model's final sentiment decisions. By using these attention maps, the system can selectively target and protect the most critical keywords, ensuring maximum defense stability while minimizing the need for unnecessary text modifications.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [29]
>>
endobj
233 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 53 0 R
/E (We also plan to expand this robustness framework to handle multi-task text mining environments, testing its capabilities on more complex datasets like IMDB and Amazon Reviews, and scaling the architecture to secure larger language models. Finally, we will focus on optimizing the token processing layers to reduce GPU memory usage, creating a highly efficient, production-ready framework capable of defending enterprise language systems from evolving adversarial threats in real-time environments.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [30]
>>
endobj
