/Title (Abstract This paper investigates the severe structural vulnerability of pre-trained Transformer architectures against semantic-preserving lexical substitutions in sentiment classification tasks. While state-of-the-art networks like DistilBERT achieve superior predictive accuracy on standard curated corpora, they exhibit catastrophic performance degradation when evaluated under localized word-level adversarial constraints. Utilizing the Stanford Sentiment Treebank \(SST-2\) benchmark dataset, we systematically expose a fine-tuned DistilBERT-base-uncased encoder to an automated Synonym Replacement Attack Framework. This framework isolates non-stopword tokens and dynamically substitutes them with lexical equivalents derived from the NLTK WordNet database. Under these baseline adversarial conditions, the model accuracy collapses to a fragile baseline stability metric of 49.00%, rendering the deep network no more reliable than a random uniform coin flip. To mitigate this optimization drift, we implement an innovative multi-epoch Adversarial Fine-Tuning Pipeline. By expanding the training distribution to incorporate a balanced matrix of both canonical inputs and dynamically perturbed synonym-injected sequences, we force the model's inner self-attention mechanisms to minimize local token-ID reliance and instead capture invariant, global semantic representations. Experimental results demonstrate a complete recovery of model stability over three optimization epochs, introducing zero degradation to clean benchmarking validation accuracy. Finally, a real-time web-based Gradio deployment layout is realized to validate the localized operational resilience of the fortified encoder network.)
/Dest [9 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 305 0 R
>>
endobj
348 0 obj
<<
/Title (Once the text has been altered, it moves directly into the Vectorized Tokenization Sub-layer, where the model applies a pre-trained DistilBERT sub-word tokenizer to convert the characters into tensor formats. The text sequence is mapped to explicit token identification integers, which are paired with attention masks and clipped to a maximum length of 128 tokens to maintain data consistency. This dual processing style ensures that the network receives a balanced mix of clean canonical entries alongside the synonym-substituted variants within each optimization batch. The input tensors are organized into parallel computational arrays, matching token identities, position encodings, and attention masks into a unified data object ready for the transformer layers.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 245 0 R
/Prev 307 0 R
/Next 349 0 R
>>
endobj
349 0 obj
<<
/Title (The operational architecture of this pipeline creates a strong defense compared to traditional, passive baseline models. While standard models evaluate inputs statically and rely on unmoving token identities, our robust framework applies an active defense strategy. During backpropagation, the loss function handles both clean and perturbed inputs simultaneously, which prevents the model's inner self-attention heads from relying on specific keyword variations. Instead, it forces the network to minimize local token dependency and distribute representation weights across the entire sentence context, establishing strong robustness against lexical drift. By tying the optimization loss to a wider distribution of word choices, the network learns to identify the emotional sentiment from the collective sentence structure rather than vulnerable keywords.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 245 0 R
/Prev 348 0 R
/Next 308 0 R
>>
endobj
350 0 obj
<<
/Title <A92054686520417574686F7228732920323032362E>
