/Title (The search for true adversarial robustness within deep language representations is a central focus of modern research, driven by the discovery that neural models optimize for surface statistical correlations rather than genuine conceptual comprehension. In the domain of sentiment analysis, early defense strategies focused primarily on input sanitization layers that acted as a barrier before the model encoder. These systems relied on spell-checking modules, vocabulary lookups, and frequency-based text filtering to catch and repair variations before they entered the computational layers. While these techniques worked well against character mutations, they failed completely when faced with synonym substitutions, as the altered words were grammatically correct and vocabulary-legal, easily passing through filters to disrupt model predictions.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 309 0 R
/Next 384 0 R
>>
endobj
352 0 obj
<<
/Title (Our work bridges this gap by introducing a streamlined, low-latency adversarial defense specifically designed for efficient deployment. Instead of relying on heavy combinatorial optimization algorithms or large external attack libraries during the training loop, we connect the model to a localized, dynamic synonym extraction pipeline driven by the hierarchical WordNet synset database. This approach allows us to achieve similar robustness gains to comprehensive adversarial training frameworks, but at a fraction of the computational cost, enabling quick convergence in fewer training iterations on accessible hardware. By combining the efficiency of distilled transformer structures with active data augmentation, our framework establishes a sustainable path toward securing real-time NLP applications.)
/Dest [10 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 309 0 R
/Prev 384 0 R
>>
endobj
